-- title:
--   Branch residues and orders at a supersingular crossing
-- statement:
--   Fix a nonzero natural number $N_0$ and a prime $q$ with $q \nmid N_0$ (`hqN`). Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $q$ in the sense of `hA`, i.e. $q$ belongs to `A.nonunits`, and let $\rho$ be a ring homomorphism from the base ring `DRLevel.R q` to $A$ whose composition with the inclusion $A \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb Q}$ is the structure map (`hρ`). Let $\mathfrak P$ be a package `DRModelPackageLevel N₀ q hqN`: its fields make `DRLevel.toBase N₀ q` (the Igusa-type morphism `IgusaScheme.igusaTo (N₀ * q) q`) proper, flat and locally of finite presentation with integral and normal source, provide a smooth proper curve model `Meta` of the level-$N_0q$ modular function field `modularFunctionFieldBar (N₀ * q)` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ together with an isomorphism `eeta` onto the generic fibre, compatible with the base map, Galois-equivariant for the arithmetic action on places, normalised on the finite Igusa $j$-chart, make the generic fibre smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, and supply cusp sections $\varepsilon_\infty,\varepsilon_0$; the remaining clauses, summarised here, furnish for every base change of `DRLevel.R q` the fibre dictionary `Mfib` (a curve model of the level-$N_0$ modular function field in characteristic $q$), the two component morphisms `comp … 0` and `comp … 1` from `DRLevel.fibre0` into the special fibre, the comparison morphism `efib` from `Mfib.C` to `DRLevel.fibre0`, and the bijection `nodeEquiv` between crossing points and supersingular places. The residue field of $A$ is assumed of characteristic $q$ and algebraically closed.
--
--   Arithmetic descent data: $O$ is a discrete valuation ring which is a domain, `eO` is a ring isomorphism of $O$ onto the valuation subring $A \cap K^{I}$ obtained by restricting $A$ along the inclusion of the fixed field $K^{I}$ of `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` (the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ of the inertia subgroup of $A$ inside its decomposition group), `hϖO` states that the maximal ideal of $O$ is generated by $q$, $\rho_O$ is a homomorphism from `DRLevel.R q` to $O$ whose composition with `eO` and with the inclusions $A\cap K^{I}\subseteq K^{I}\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}$ is the structure map (`hρO`), and `toκ` is a homomorphism from $O$ to the residue field of $A$ which by `htoκ` sends $o$ to the residue class of the image of `eO o` in $A$.
--
--   Modular and reduction data: `data` is a `ModularPolynomialData q`, that is a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb Z[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ annihilating the pair $(j, j_q)$ of $q$-expansions; `hKr` is the Kronecker congruence, asserting that the reduction of $\Phi$ modulo $q$ is the product of $Y^{q} - X$ and $Y - X^{q}$, where $Y$ denotes the outer variable and $X$ the variable of the coefficient ring; `hα` and `hβ` assert the integrality of the ring homomorphisms underlying the Hecke operators $\bar\alpha$, $\bar\beta$ at level $N_0$ and prime $q$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$. Further, `fm` is a `CharPModel.FibreModel N₀ A q` for the residue field of $A$ with its residue map: it consists of two subrings `BFin`, `BInf` of the base-changed level-$N_0$ Laurent field containing the constants from $A$ and the relevant powers of $\bar j$, integral over the affine bases, together with specialisation homomorphisms `piFin`, `piInf` into `modularFunctionFieldC` over the residue field satisfying the listed compatibilities; `cc` is the cusp-chart property that $\bar j_{N_0}\bar j^{-N_0}$ lies in `BInf` and specialises as expected; `hfin` and `hinf` say that every element of `IgusaScheme.chartAlgFin N₀ q`, respectively of `IgusaScheme.chartAlgInf N₀ q`, lies in `fm.BFin`, respectively in `fm.BInf`, after the coefficient embedding; `hred` says the residue map of $A$ is surjective; `dataAll` provides modular polynomial data for every divisor of $N_0$; `hsepΦ` says that the level-$N_0$ modular polynomial, reduced into the residue field and viewed in the rational function field, is separable. Next, $P$ is a `PlaceSpecialization` for $A$, $q$, $N_0$, the given `data`, `hKr`, the residue field with its residue map and the Hecke integralities: it carries a specialisation map `sp` of places from the level-$N_0$ function field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ to the level-$N_0$ function field in characteristic $q$, an induced homomorphism on degree-zero divisor classes, and the compatibility clauses for $j$, $j_{N_0}$, the cusps and the Hecke correspondences (many clauses, summarised here); `hP` identifies `P.sp` with the specialisation `fm.spPlace hred dataAll hsepΦ` constructed from the fibre model. Finally $R$ is a `ProlongationTuple` over $P$: a residue homomorphism `redBar` and an embedding $\iota$ of the level-$N_0$ function field over the residue field of $A$, two regular prolongations $R_1$, $R_2$ of $A$ to the level-$N_0q$ function field with residue field the full level-$N_0$ function field over the residue field of $A$, and their compatibility clauses (including the Atkin–Lehner comparison of the two residues); `hR` is `R.IsModel`, the conjunction of the two divisor laws and the two cusp laws at $\infty$ and at $0$.
--
--   Geometric data over $O$: write $X_O$ for the pullback of `DRLevel.toBase N₀ q` along `Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom ρO)`. It is assumed integral (`hint`), and the special fibre `DRLevel.fibre0` of level $N_0$ along `toκ.comp ρO` is assumed integral (`hfib0`). A point $x$ of $X_O$ is given, together with a ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the function field of $X_O$ to `modularFunctionFieldBar (N₀ * q)` subject to two normalisations: `hφO` says that for every $a \in O$ the $\varphi$-image of the germ at $x$ of the global function determined by $a$ is the constant given by the image of $a$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ through `eO` and the inclusions above, and `hφj` says that for every element $a$ of `IgusaScheme.chartAlgFin (N₀ * q) q` the $\varphi$-image of the germ at the generic point of the preimage of the finite Igusa chart of the section determined by $a$ is, as a Laurent series, the coefficient embedding `coeffEmb` of the Laurent expansion of $a$; the chart preimage is assumed nonempty (`hne`).
--
--   The crossing: $n$ is a point of the pullback of `𝔓.comp (ResidueField ↥A) (toκ.comp ρO) 0` and `𝔓.comp (ResidueField ↥A) (toκ.comp ρO) 1`, and $P_0, P_1$ are closed points of `(𝔓.Mfib (ResidueField ↥A) (toκ.comp ρO)).C`. The hypotheses `hP₀` and `hP₁` say that `efib` carries $P_0$, respectively $P_1$, to the image of $n$ under the first, respectively the second, projection; `hx₀` and `hx₁` say that $x$ is the image of $P_0$ under `efib` followed by `comp … 0` followed by `DRLevel.bcMap ρO toκ`, and likewise the image of $P_1$ under `efib` followed by `comp … 1` followed by `DRLevel.bcMap ρO toκ`; `hsp₀` and `hsp₁` say that the images under `comp … 0 ≫ DRLevel.bcMap ρO toκ`, respectively `comp … 1 ≫ DRLevel.bcMap ρO toκ`, of the generic point of `DRLevel.fibre0` specialise to $x$. Further, $w$ is a place of the level-$N_0$ modular function field over the residue field of $A$ lying in `ssPlaces q N₀` (a supersingular place, `hw`), and `hn` identifies the image of $n$ under `𝔓.nodeEquiv` with $w$.
--
--   Orientation and integrality at the node: $t_F$ is a germ in the stalk of $X_O$ at $x$ whose image in the function field satisfies $\varphi(t_F) =$ `jQFun N₀ q` $-$ `jFun N₀ q`$^{q}$, the difference of $j(q^{q})$ and $j(q)^{q}$ in the level-$N_0q$ function field (`htF`), and `hor` requires that the image of $t_F$ under the specialisation map of stalks attached to `hsp₀` lie in the maximal ideal of the stalk at the generic point of the component labelled $0$; thus that component is the one on which $\varphi(t_F)$ vanishes generically. Finally `hconv` requires that for every germ $s$ in the stalk at $x$ the element $\varphi(s)$ lie in `R.nodeIntegers w`, the subring of the level-$N_0q$ function field of those $f$ that are integral for $R_1$ and for $R_2$ and lie in the valuation subring of every place $V$ of that field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with `P.reduceFst V = w`.
--
--   Conclusion: for every germ $s$ in the stalk of $X_O$ at $x$, the following four assertions hold.
--
--   First, `R.nodeResidue₁ w` applied to $\varphi(s)$, taken with the membership supplied by `hconv`, vanishes if and only if $s$ lies in the preimage, under the stalk specialisation map attached to `hsp₀`, of the maximal ideal of the stalk at the generic point of the component labelled $0$.
--
--   Second, `R.nodeResidue₂ w` applied to $\varphi(s)$ vanishes if and only if $s$ lies in the preimage, under the stalk specialisation map attached to `hsp₁`, of the maximal ideal of the stalk at the generic point of the component labelled $1$.
--
--   Third, the order `Place.ord` of the first node residue of $\varphi(s)$ at $w$ — that is, minus the logarithm of its adic valuation at $w$ — equals the order, at the place `placeOfPoint P₀` of the fibre dictionary `𝔓.Mfib`, of the element of the level-$N_0$ modular function field over the residue field obtained from $s$ by transporting along the stalk identification given by `hx₀`, applying the stalk map at $P_0$ of `efib` followed by `comp … 0` followed by `DRLevel.bcMap ρO toκ`, passing to the function field of `Mfib.C`, and applying the inverse of `Mfib.ffEquiv`.
--
--   Fourth, the order of the second node residue of $\varphi(s)$ at the translate `arithFrobC q (ResidueField ↥A) N₀ • w` of $w$ by the arithmetic Frobenius semilinear automorphism, which acts on Laurent coefficients by $x \mapsto x^{q}$, equals the order, at the place `placeOfPoint P₁`, of the element obtained from $s$ in the same way through `hx₁`, the stalk map at $P_1$ of `efib` followed by `comp … 1` followed by `DRLevel.bcMap ρO toκ`, and the inverse of `Mfib.ffEquiv`.
--
--   This is the residue-matching step at a supersingular crossing of the Deligne–Rapoport model of the modular curve of level $N_0q$ over the discrete valuation ring $O$: it identifies the vanishing loci of the two branch residues on the node ring with the two component primes at $x$, and matches their orders with the orders of the restricted germ on the two copies of the level-$N_0$ curve in characteristic $q$, the second branch being read after translation by the arithmetic Frobenius. It is used by [`ModularCurve.DRModelPackageLevel.exists_nodeCoordinates_and_forall_mem_support_iff_chainPos_of_chartPresentation`](thm.html#ModularCurve.DRModelPackageLevel.exists_nodeCoordinates_and_forall_mem_support_iff_chainPos_of_chartPresentation) in the analysis of the special fibre that underlies level lowering at $q$.
